│ Task: OWASP A05 Injection: SQL, NoSQL, command, template, LDAP and header/log injection, plus      │
│ cross-site scripting, using safe detection before any impactful proof.                             │
│                                                                                                    │
│ Phase: Identify injection candidates                                                               │
│ Objective: Find where input reaches an interpreter.                                                │
│ Techniques:                                                                                        │
│   1. Work from the inventory: every parameter, header, cookie and body field is a candidate,       │
│ including those only the client normally sets.                                                     │
│   2. Note where input is reflected in responses, stored and rendered later, or appears to          │
│ influence a query, path, command or template.                                                      │
│   3. Prefer detection that is safe and reversible: syntax that would provoke a parse difference or │
│ an error, rather than anything destructive.                                                        │
│                                                                                                    │
│ Phase: Confirm by differential behaviour                                                           │
│ Objective: Prove interpretation, not coincidence.                                                  │
│ Techniques:                                                                                        │
│   1. Confirm an injection by demonstrating a controlled difference between two inputs - an error   │
│ that appears and disappears, a boolean condition that changes the result, a timing difference you  │
│ can repeat. A single odd response is not proof.                                                    │
│   2. For cross-site scripting, show the payload is stored or reflected AND rendered in a context   │
│ where it would execute. Quote the response showing the unescaped payload.                          │
│   3. Do not run destructive payloads, do not modify or drop data, and do not attempt command       │
│ execution beyond a harmless, clearly identifiable proof.                                           │
│   4. For claimed code or command execution the proof is OUTPUT of a command you chose, returned by │
│ the target - not a payload you sent and not an error that looks interesting. Match the payload to  │
│ the actual platform.                                                                               │
│                                                                                                    │
│ Phase: Bound the impact                                                                            │
│ Objective: State what the injection actually reaches.                                              │
│ Techniques:                                                                                        │
│   1. Establish what the injection can read or affect, and whether it crosses a trust boundary.     │
│   2. Run the control test: the same request with benign input, reported alongside.                 │
│                                                                                                    │
│ Phase: Verify before reporting                                                                     │
│ Objective: Apply the same evidence standard to every finding, so results are comparable across     │
│ categories.                                                                                        │
│ Techniques:                                                                                        │
│   1. PROOF DISCIPLINE - label every finding DEMONSTRATED (you executed it and the response proves  │
│ it, quote that response), INFERRED (follows from evidence but was not executed), or UNVERIFIED     │
│ (suspected from a version, component or code pattern only). Identifying software is never a        │
│ demonstration; never write 'confirmed' without having executed something.                          │
│   2. RUN THE CONTROL TEST. Before claiming any access or bypass, repeat the request WITHOUT the    │
│ credential, token, header or material you believe is responsible. If the result is the same, you   │
│ have proven nothing - the resource was already reachable. State the control result alongside the   │
│ finding. This single omission is the most common source of false critical findings.                │
│   3. BUDGET DISCIPLINE - rank leads by evidence strength (material in hand > observed behaviour >  │
│ suspicion from a version string) and give each a step budget. STOP RULE: about 8-10 consecutive    │
│ steps on one lead with no new evidence means move on, recording what you tried.                    │
│   4. Record findings to notes as you go, with the endpoint, the request, the response and the      │
│ control result, so later phases and the report can use them.                                       │
│   5. Stay non-destructive: prefer read-only proof, do not modify or delete data, do not lock       │
│ accounts, and stop once the point is demonstrated.                                                 │
│                                                                                                    │
│                                                                                                    │
